{"analysis":"Three candidate platforms are evaluated against the selection criteria for a routing-focused agent system (RefractRouter v0.1): DeepAgents, LangGraph, and DeepSeek Harness (DSH), with Pydantic AI considered as an optional dependency. The primary criteria, derived from the evidence, are: (1) ability to enforce a fixed task DAG for per-node model selection, (2) controlled agent autonomy (no dynamic planning or sub-agent generation in the core loop), (3) integration fit with a LangGraph-based execution substrate, (4) typed/structured output support, and (5) separation between core routing and outer validation environments.

DeepAgents (source_001) is built on LangGraph and offers planning, sub-agents, a filesystem abstraction, and human-in-the-loop. Its strength is rich agent capabilities, but the tradeoff is that autonomous planning and dynamic sub-agent generation must be explicitly disabled to keep the DAG fixed and ensure per-node model selection is the router's responsibility rather than the harness's. LangGraph (source_002) provides explicit nodes, edges, durable state, and checkpointing, which directly supports a fixed task DAG and is already the lower-level execution substrate. The tradeoff is that it is a lower-level framework and lacks the higher-level agent ergonomics of DeepAgents, so additional harness code may be needed. DSH (source_004) uses a pluggable agent loop, but for v0.1 it is reserved for the outer validation environment and is excluded from the core routing loop to avoid contaminating latency, cost, and quality measurements with its own agent-loop overhead. Pydantic AI (source_003) is conditionally included only if structured-output parsing becomes a bottleneck, trading added dependency weight for stronger typing guarantees.

Key tradeoffs: DeepAgents gives the fastest path to a working harness but risks DAG drift if autonomy is not fully disabled; LangGraph gives the cleanest fixed-DAG substrate but requires more glue code; DSH offers a strong validation harness but is deliberately out of the critical path; Pydantic AI is deferred to keep the v0.1 dependency surface minimal. The benchmark (source_007) must report quality, cost, and critical-path latency on fixed DAGs, so any platform that introduces non-deterministic planning or extra loops in the core path is disfavored. The scoring rubric (source_008) further reinforces deterministic, well-structured outputs, favoring platforms that can be constrained to produce stable, parseable results.","evidence":[{"source_id":"source_001","title":"DeepAgents","content_hash":"ed7d5a479cc077720b641eb8ecf4a25c3af32701fc8403138c57532c6dd03d39","claim":"DeepAgents is a LangChain harness built on LangGraph, providing planning, sub-agents, a filesystem abstraction, and human-in-the-loop controls."},{"source_id":"source_001","title":"DeepAgents","content_hash":"ed7d5a479cc077720b641eb8ecf4a25c3af32701fc8403138c57532c6dd03d39","claim":"For RefractRouter v0.1, DeepAgents can serve as the fixed-DAG execution harness if its autonomous planning and dynamic sub-agent generation remain disabled."},{"source_id":"source_002","title":"LangGraph","content_hash":"97960ed618df652ae16f900a1ea677e257684118f5eab68686b2f8713ae74960","claim":"LangGraph is a graph state machine framework supporting explicit nodes, edges, durable state, and checkpointing, used by RefractRouter as the lower-level execution substrate for a fixed task DAG."},{"source_id":"source_003","title":"Pydantic AI","content_hash":"7edd7c4a831f8cb06e0c9fc8b17911818ad63ff686b7d7245b176c46a3cea42e","claim":"Pydantic AI provides typed tool definitions and structured model outputs and is only needed as a core dependency for RefractRouter v0.1 if structured-output parsing becomes a bottleneck."},{"source_id":"source_004","title":"DeepSeek Harness","content_hash":"b06d562fa3e636b8ffb02c1578e300033ae7f23672b90445a9489bbe65b57331","claim":"DeepSeek Harness (DSH) is an agent harness in which the agent loop itself is a plugin."},{"source_id":"source_004","title":"DeepSeek Harness","content_hash":"b06d562fa3e636b8ffb02c1578e300033ae7f23672b90445a9489bbe65b57331","claim":"For RefractRouter v0.1, DSH is reserved for the outer validation environment and is not used in the core routing loop."},{"source_id":"source_005","title":"LLM routing research gap","content_hash":"418b6cda722a8338f10fa145fb4f4f5c3f188ff413651e4d73aa2949b4e07460","claim":"Existing LLM routing work mostly selects one model for an entire request, whereas RefractRouter investigates per-node model selection within a task DAG to improve the quality-cost-latency Pareto frontier."},{"source_id":"source_006","title":"Task decomposition boundary","content_hash":"96a837f9788f5d0c6d9ab2cc9633775c42dc5569afc70b1782dc1d6d7a198504","claim":"Static task decomposition belongs to the application layer; the router receives the DAG as input and focuses on per-node model selection, with dynamic re-splitting deferred until node-level routing is validated."},{"source_id":"source_007","title":"Benchmark design","content_hash":"03e9443028f0065ae41218c422efdcebe73225465468098c9ba49f03596be4d0","claim":"A useful routing benchmark requires fixed task DAGs, multiple candidate models, per-node and per-task scoring, cost accounting, and critical-path latency, and the first benchmark should stay small enough to reproduce."},{"source_id":"source_008","title":"Scoring rubric","content_hash":"b669e75265f9d0997a8f62659ca6526b2911a6d4af1f2c9ed956870c166a816e","claim":"The v0.1 scoring rubric weights requirement coverage, evidence accuracy, analysis depth, structure and readability, and HTML validity, with scoring prompts and rubric versions versioned alongside run records."}]}
